1
00:00:00,513 --> 00:00:03,706
So the model file concept is an important

2
00:00:03,786 --> 00:00:06,433
concept. It allows you to build customized

3
00:00:06,433 --> 00:00:06,979
versions

4
00:00:07,556 --> 00:00:09,225
based on existing models.

5
00:00:10,059 --> 00:00:12,466
You can also take it a step further though.

6
00:00:13,092 --> 00:00:16,445
You can technically also build new models

7
00:00:16,638 --> 00:00:17,697
with a model file

8
00:00:18,242 --> 00:00:21,515
that are not based on base models from the

9
00:00:21,595 --> 00:00:22,590
Ollama catalog.

10
00:00:23,938 --> 00:00:27,275
Instead, you could have a GGUF file, which

11
00:00:27,323 --> 00:00:29,441
is a file format that stores stores these

12
00:00:29,634 --> 00:00:32,409
parameters, these weights, along with some

13
00:00:32,441 --> 00:00:33,083
metadata.

14
00:00:33,788 --> 00:00:36,580
And you could get such a GGUF file because

15
00:00:36,596 --> 00:00:39,708
you train the model yourself or from Hugging

16
00:00:39,804 --> 00:00:40,029
Face.

17
00:00:40,783 --> 00:00:41,665
Now, for example, here

18
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:46,301
I have the QuEN-3 model, or to be precise, a

19
00:00:46,301 --> 00:00:47,889
quantized version of that model.

20
00:00:48,563 --> 00:00:51,338
It's a normal model shared on Hugging Face.

21
00:00:52,092 --> 00:00:54,434
And that is a model that is indeed already

22
00:00:54,434 --> 00:00:55,621
supported by Ollama.

23
00:00:56,198 --> 00:00:59,053
So you do find Quentn 3 in their model

24
00:00:59,069 --> 00:00:59,839
catalog as well.

25
00:01:00,577 --> 00:01:02,469
But if it would not be included there,

26
00:01:03,303 --> 00:01:05,324
and for some models on Hugging Face, that

27
00:01:05,324 --> 00:01:07,633
might be the case, because whilst most

28
00:01:07,633 --> 00:01:09,590
models and definitely all important and

29
00:01:09,654 --> 00:01:10,808
popular models are

30
00:01:11,562 --> 00:01:13,952
part of the Ollama model catalog, some

31
00:01:14,064 --> 00:01:15,171
models might not be.

32
00:01:15,988 --> 00:01:17,833
And therefore, for such situations, you

33
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:21,008
could download such a GGUF file from Hugging

34
00:01:21,104 --> 00:01:23,529
Face. So you could download the model, the

35
00:01:23,529 --> 00:01:25,954
parameters from Hugging Face, and then use

36
00:01:26,083 --> 00:01:30,114
that downloaded GGUF file as a base to set

37
00:01:30,114 --> 00:01:32,636
up your own Ollama-compatible model.

38
00:01:33,792 --> 00:01:35,494
How do you do that? Well, here for this

39
00:01:35,494 --> 00:01:37,181
example, and you'll find a link to that

40
00:01:37,181 --> 00:01:38,594
attached, there is

41
00:01:39,574 --> 00:01:42,529
a list of download links here for different

42
00:01:42,561 --> 00:01:44,778
quantized versions of that model. And I want

43
00:01:44,778 --> 00:01:47,572
to use the Q4KM version here.

44
00:01:48,567 --> 00:01:50,732
And in general, as you also see here, it's

45
00:01:50,732 --> 00:01:53,474
recommended to go for the Q4 versions, the

46
00:01:53,474 --> 00:01:56,521
4-bit quantized versions, and then KM or KS.

47
00:01:57,403 --> 00:01:59,728
These typically give you the best trade-off

48
00:01:59,728 --> 00:02:02,743
between quality and performance and reducing

49
00:02:02,759 --> 00:02:03,897
hardware requirements.

50
00:02:04,683 --> 00:02:06,656
And you can download this file here

51
00:02:07,425 --> 00:02:08,917
directly from Hugging Face.

52
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:11,402
And again, this is just an example. You

53
00:02:11,418 --> 00:02:13,118
could also have a model that you trained

54
00:02:13,118 --> 00:02:14,289
yourself, of course.

55
00:02:15,274 --> 00:02:17,494
And then you could create a model file next

56
00:02:17,591 --> 00:02:20,020
to that downloaded model. So next to this

57
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:22,674
downloaded GGUF file, which is a file that

58
00:02:22,674 --> 00:02:24,493
contains all these parameters and weights

59
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:25,974
and some metadata.

60
00:02:26,618 --> 00:02:28,454
And now with the from instruction, you would

61
00:02:28,551 --> 00:02:30,435
point at that downloaded file.

62
00:02:31,369 --> 00:02:33,205
So you would use that file name including

63
00:02:33,221 --> 00:02:34,076
the file extension

64
00:02:35,641 --> 00:02:37,126
and specify it like this here.

65
00:02:38,207 --> 00:02:40,858
And now you would build a brand new model or

66
00:02:40,858 --> 00:02:42,947
you would define a brand new model based on

67
00:02:42,979 --> 00:02:45,376
that downloaded model, based on these

68
00:02:45,489 --> 00:02:46,526
parameters and weights.

69
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:49,538
Of course, you can still add a system

70
00:02:49,538 --> 00:02:51,007
message and parameters,

71
00:02:52,412 --> 00:02:54,560
but you also might want to specify a

72
00:02:54,592 --> 00:02:55,141
template.

73
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:58,807
Now, the great thing about GGUF files is

74
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:01,360
that, as I explained, they do not just

75
00:03:01,425 --> 00:03:04,140
contain the parameters but also some

76
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:04,657
metadata.

77
00:03:05,449 --> 00:03:07,534
And one important piece of metadata they do

78
00:03:07,566 --> 00:03:10,613
include is the chat template, which in case

79
00:03:10,613 --> 00:03:13,118
of Quen 3 is rather complex, as you see.

80
00:03:14,387 --> 00:03:15,703
You can also see that

81
00:03:16,233 --> 00:03:18,240
on the Quen 3 page of Ollama.

82
00:03:19,059 --> 00:03:20,809
There, if you open the template, you got a

83
00:03:20,809 --> 00:03:22,624
rather complex template there as well.

84
00:03:24,037 --> 00:03:25,578
By the way, these template formats are

85
00:03:25,642 --> 00:03:26,092
different.

86
00:03:27,071 --> 00:03:29,592
Ollama uses the Go templating language.

87
00:03:30,363 --> 00:03:32,852
On Hugging Face here, that is a different

88
00:03:32,852 --> 00:03:33,205
language.

89
00:03:34,170 --> 00:03:36,986
So you would need to use that Go templating

90
00:03:37,051 --> 00:03:39,592
language expected by Ollama if you were to

91
00:03:39,592 --> 00:03:40,644
set up your own template.

92
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:43,915
Now I'll first show you what happens if you

93
00:03:43,931 --> 00:03:46,093
don't specify a template though. So I'll

94
00:03:46,271 --> 00:03:48,917
just set up a basic model file that just has

95
00:03:48,949 --> 00:03:51,030
this from instruction and nothing else. And

96
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:52,656
of course we could add a system prompt

97
00:03:52,656 --> 00:03:54,296
parameters, but I won't add anything.

98
00:03:55,774 --> 00:03:58,748
And I'll then create my own Quen model

99
00:03:58,764 --> 00:04:01,126
version based on that model file with ollama

100
00:04:01,126 --> 00:04:01,631
create.

101
00:04:03,886 --> 00:04:05,061
And if I now run this

102
00:04:06,543 --> 00:04:07,623
and I ask it something,

103
00:04:08,557 --> 00:04:10,329
I get a super weird response.

104
00:04:14,664 --> 00:04:17,077
And I got this weird response by the model

105
00:04:17,515 --> 00:04:20,834
because it had no structure at all. It had

106
00:04:20,834 --> 00:04:24,059
no template. It did not understand my

107
00:04:24,059 --> 00:04:25,634
message I sent to it in the end.

108
00:04:26,694 --> 00:04:29,489
The message I sent to it was not structured

109
00:04:29,602 --> 00:04:30,727
in a way, was not wrapped with the right

110
00:04:30,727 --> 00:04:31,819
identifiers. It knew from the text,

111
00:04:33,836 --> 00:04:34,622
training process.

112
00:04:35,456 --> 00:04:37,285
Now, of course, I can use the chat template

113
00:04:37,285 --> 00:04:39,868
here from Hugging Face and try to use that.

114
00:04:39,949 --> 00:04:42,355
But as mentioned, this must be adjusted to

115
00:04:42,355 --> 00:04:44,409
use the Golang templating language.

116
00:04:45,003 --> 00:04:46,447
And whilst you could, of course, try to

117
00:04:46,447 --> 00:04:49,174
convert this manually or with help of AI,

118
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:52,624
here I'll take the easy route and I'll just

119
00:04:52,624 --> 00:04:54,886
use the one that was built by Ollama. And of

120
00:04:54,934 --> 00:04:56,651
course, in reality, I would use the entire

121
00:04:56,795 --> 00:04:59,619
model that was published by Ollama. This is

122
00:04:59,667 --> 00:05:01,769
just a demo here, but here I'll just use

123
00:05:01,769 --> 00:05:02,475
their template.

124
00:05:03,444 --> 00:05:04,635
Copy all of that,

125
00:05:05,697 --> 00:05:07,949
go back to my model file and add such a

126
00:05:07,949 --> 00:05:08,368
template.

127
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:10,411
Add the triple quotes

128
00:05:11,505 --> 00:05:14,477
to insert a multiline template and insert

129
00:05:14,477 --> 00:05:15,317
this here

130
00:05:16,739 --> 00:05:17,111
like this.

131
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:20,606
Now you can ignore the fact that something's

132
00:05:20,606 --> 00:05:22,318
red here. This should work.

133
00:05:23,780 --> 00:05:27,536
So now with that template added, I'll first

134
00:05:27,536 --> 00:05:30,419
of all remove my model, my broken model.

135
00:05:31,375 --> 00:05:32,807
And then I'll repeat the command for

136
00:05:32,823 --> 00:05:33,434
creating it,

137
00:05:34,030 --> 00:05:36,217
now based on this updated model file, which

138
00:05:36,266 --> 00:05:38,019
does now include a proper template.

139
00:05:39,252 --> 00:05:41,276
And if I now run this updated model

140
00:05:41,864 --> 00:05:43,513
and I ask, how are you?

141
00:05:45,678 --> 00:05:48,399
I get a more reasonable output. Now this

142
00:05:48,592 --> 00:05:50,733
model, the QWEN model, is actually a

143
00:05:50,765 --> 00:05:53,341
reasoning model which tries to think first

144
00:05:53,341 --> 00:05:55,371
before it responds, which is why we see this

145
00:05:55,371 --> 00:05:55,641
block.

146
00:05:56,326 --> 00:05:58,652
But it generally understood my message and

147
00:05:58,684 --> 00:06:00,240
it gave a reasonable response.

148
00:06:01,250 --> 00:06:03,239
So that's working the way it should.

149
00:06:04,442 --> 00:06:06,335
And that therefore does not just show you

150
00:06:06,431 --> 00:06:09,816
how you can set up models from scratch just

151
00:06:09,816 --> 00:06:12,719
based on such a GGUF file, for example, but

152
00:06:12,719 --> 00:06:15,077
it also shows you the importance of adding

153
00:06:15,414 --> 00:06:16,040
templates.

154
00:06:16,810 --> 00:06:19,024
Again, because it's important, you don't

155
00:06:19,024 --> 00:06:21,045
need those when using the models that are

156
00:06:21,045 --> 00:06:24,077
published in the Ollama model catalog. And

157
00:06:24,077 --> 00:06:26,007
you also don't need to specify templates

158
00:06:26,023 --> 00:06:28,000
when building your own customized versions

159
00:06:28,113 --> 00:06:29,608
based off those models.

160
00:06:30,284 --> 00:06:32,374
But if you build a model from scratch, as I

161
00:06:32,374 --> 00:06:34,963
just showed you, then these templates do

162
00:06:34,963 --> 00:06:35,397
matter.
